2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160250Z   try <expr> catch {
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160410Z     err => {
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160540Z       <cleanup code>
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160700Z       raise err
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160830Z     }
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4160970Z   }
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4161050Z 
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4161640Z If <cleanup code> does not mention `err` or perform `return`/`break`/`continue`, rewrite to:
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4161940Z 
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4162020Z   errdefer <cleanup code>
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4162180Z   <expr>
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4162260Z 
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4162760Z Note: `try ... catch` will no longer capture async cancellation in the future, so using `catch` for cleanup is dangerous for async code.
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4163550Z If your handler need to manipulate or inspect `err`, but contain cleanup code as well,
2026-11-06T15:36:05.4164260Z it is still recommended to move the cleanup part to `errdefer` as well, leaving only the error inspecting part in `catch`.
